body:
kalide's source keeps an abstraction only where it earns its place, and the simplification is evidence-backed: a helper whose call graph shows a single caller and whose body only delegates to another function — adding no behaviour of its own — is folded back into that caller, and a function is split into helpers only where the pieces are reused or independently tested. A one-caller helper stays when it names a distinct step of a long function, is independently tested, or is a function value or interface member; the evidence is the call graph (a single incoming `Calls` edge) plus inspection.

AC:
- internal/slide's `pathSegments` and `instanceIndexes` collapse to one name — each body is `return parseChain(path).indexes` and `parseSections` is the only caller of each — and `parseSections` calls the survivor;
- no pass-through helper with a single caller remains that only delegates to another function and adds no behaviour of its own;
- the build and the internal/slide tests pass.
